From 293495139f8dd890f05cef6fb43fb83a3ddf5a89 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 24 Aug 2026 09:55:14 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Video: pick the model, and price it honestly
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A superadmin chose ONE text-to-video model and ONE image-to-video model
for the whole platform, and the studio offered a 3-30 second duration
slider the provider layer then silently clamped: Kling snapped to 5 or
10, MiniMax ignored duration entirely, Veo got a minimal payload. A
7-second request came back at 5 and the length pass padded it by
freezing the final frame. The user was never told.

The capability catalog
  lib/videoGen/modelCatalog.ts is now the single source of truth for
  what each model can do — modes, durations, aspect ratios, resolutions,
  quality switch, native audio — and three things read it: the studio,
  which renders only the controls a model supports; preflight, which
  refuses an impossible request BEFORE funding; and the provider
  adapters, which stop sniffing model-name substrings.

  Seventeen models across Replicate and OpenRouter. Deliberately curated
  rather than exhaustive: every model is a price row, a QA surface and a
  support burden.

Model choice
  POST /ai/generate-video takes modelId, resolution, quality and
  generateAudio. GET /ai/video-models returns what this workspace may
  actually pick — the admin's allowlist, filtered to providers whose key
  is saved, since offering a model preflight is about to refuse is worse
  than not offering it. Admins curate the list from the existing video
  gen settings screen (video_gen_settings.enabled_model_ids, NULL = all).

Billing
  A picked model carries a tier multiplier — draft 1x, standard 2x,
  premium 4x — applied to the GENERATION count, so a four-shot premium
  clip is sixteen units, because it really is sixteen premium
  generations' worth of provider spend.

  NO modelId means multiplier 1. Every existing job, and every job that
  leaves the model alone, costs exactly what it always did; the whole
  feature is opt-in on both axes.

  Both funding rails stay consistent because everything that recomputes
  a price goes through videoJobUnits(engine, options): the route's
  reservation, the runner's usage rows and refunds, the sweep, storyboard
  discard. The scene-insert route now reserves the multiplier too — a
  flat unit there against a multiplied recomputation elsewhere is how a
  refund quietly hands back more than was taken.

  Wallet workspaces are unaffected by the bucket: they reserve an
  estimate and settle at the real provider cost from the admin price
  catalog, exactly as before. Resolution deliberately does NOT change
  the unit price — a cheaper tier is a faster render, not a cheaper one.

Also: normalizeVideo encodes at the requested resolution instead of
upscaling every clip to 1080-class and calling it 1080p, and Veo finally
receives generate_audio, which is the whole reason to reach for it.

Migration: one additive nullable column (video_gen_settings
.enabled_model_ids) plus optional JSON fields on existing options —
pnpm --filter @workspace/db run push, no data loss.

Verified: pnpm run typecheck clean; api-server 2256/2257 (the one
failure, ads.meta-reconnect, fails identically on 8138eab without a Meta
app credential in env); web 838/838; spec lints, codegen drift clean.

